# rc-launcher settings (Linux, systemd --user) -- every key this fork reads, with this
# fork's values. The live file is ~/.config/rc-launcher/rc-launcher.env (0600). Either:
#   - let install.sh write it: every RC_X=value you pass (RC_SPAWN=worktree ./install.sh)
#     lands there and survives re-runs; or
#   - install -m 600 deploy/rc-launcher.env.example ~/.config/rc-launcher/rc-launcher.env,
#     edit it, then run ./install.sh (it keeps your values).
# After editing the live file by hand:  systemctl --user restart rc-launcher
#
# Format (systemd EnvironmentFile): KEY=value, one per line. No quotes, no spaces, no
# $expansion, and no comment after a value (a '#' there would become part of the value).
# A leading ~ is fine: the code expands it.
#
# Read by rc-launcher.service and rc-healthcheck.service only. NOT by rc-tmux.service, so
# none of this (the ntfy topic, say) leaks into the Claude sessions' environment.
#
# Deliberately NOT settable here:
#   the token          ~/.config/rc-launcher/token (0600); never in an env var, unit or repo
#   RC_LAUNCHER_BIND   pinned to 127.0.0.1 in rc-launcher.service (the host has a public IP;
#                      `tailscale serve` is the only way in)
#   RC_TMUX_BIN        pinned to deploy/rc-tmux, the client for the dedicated tmux server
#                      rc-tmux.service owns (exact -S socket; never your personal server)
#   RC_TMUX_SOCKET     not used by this deployment (the shim above already pins the socket);
#                      leave it unset if the code grows it
# install.sh drops the first two from this file (with a note) if they appear.

## Projects and sessions ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The directory whose subdirectories are the projects the phone can launch.
RC_PROJECTS_PARENT=~/workspace
# Optional two-level layout: category dirs under the parent, comma-separated (e.g. work,oss).
# RC_PROJECT_GROUPS=
# Optional extra roots outside the parent, comma/colon-separated absolute paths.
# RC_PROJECT_ROOTS=
# Model every launched session is pinned to (--model), fresh and resumed alike.
RC_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
# same-dir | worktree | session. worktree gives each phone session its own git worktree
# (needs a git repo; resume/--continue does not apply). Upstream marks worktree/session as
# unverified on current Claude Code builds: check the first launch.
RC_SPAWN=worktree
# continue | fork | off: reopen the project's last thread (same-dir only).
RC_RESUME=continue
# 1 = close the project's desktop claude session before a phone launch takes over its thread.
RC_TAKEOVER=1
# 1 = git-checkpoint the working tree before each remote turn.
# RC_SNAPSHOT=
# Absolute path of the claude binary (the service PATH has no shell profile).
RC_CLAUDE_BIN=~/.local/bin/claude
# Loopback port the launcher listens on; tailscale serve proxies to it.
RC_LAUNCHER_PORT=8787

## Tailnet guards (web tier) ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Set both after deploy/tailscale-serve.sh prints them. The token stays the real gate; these
# refuse a request that did not come through tailscale serve for this name and your login.
# Host header allowlist: the node's MagicDNS name (add name:8443 if you serve on 8443).
# RC_ALLOWED_HOSTS=<host>.<tailnet>.ts.net
# Tailscale-User-Login allowlist (tailscale serve sets this header), comma-separated.
# RC_TAILSCALE_USERS=you@example.com

## File share (/files) ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Off unless you opt in: a token holder can read, upload and replace files in the share.
RC_SHARE_ENABLED=0
RC_SHARE_DIR=~/rc-share

## Alerts (rc-healthcheck.timer, every 30 min) ----------------------------------------------
# A headless server has no desktop notifications: without this, alerts only reach the
# journal (journalctl --user -u rc-healthcheck -p warning). ntfy topic or any http(s)
# webhook; the topic name is the secret, so make it long and random, e.g.
#   echo "https://ntfy.sh/rc-$(openssl rand -hex 16)"
# RC_NOTIFY_URL=https://ntfy.sh/rc-<long-random-suffix>
